WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.234 --> 00:00:02.535
-Our next guest
is a talented actress

00:00:02.535 --> 00:00:05.171
you know from movies such as
"Downsizing," "The Menu,"

00:00:05.171 --> 00:00:06.238
and the limited series
"Watchmen"

00:00:06.238 --> 00:00:07.774
and "Big Little Lies."

00:00:07.774 --> 00:00:10.142
She stars in "The Whale," which
is in select theaters tomorrow,

00:00:10.142 --> 00:00:12.111
and opens nationwide
December 21st.

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:13.445
Let's take a look.

00:00:13.445 --> 00:00:15.381
-I really hate you for putting
me through this again,

00:00:15.381 --> 00:00:16.683
you know that?

00:00:19.085 --> 00:00:22.155
Those last few months
before Alan,

00:00:22.155 --> 00:00:24.690
I'd come over here, shake him,
scream at him,

00:00:24.690 --> 00:00:26.559
just trying to get him
to [ Bleep ] eat something.

00:00:26.559 --> 00:00:27.926
God, that was awful.

00:00:27.926 --> 00:00:29.428
-It was awful for me too.

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:30.663
-Yeah, well you weren't the one

00:00:30.663 --> 00:00:32.731
who had to identify
his body all bloated.

00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:35.401
-They wouldn't let me.

00:00:35.401 --> 00:00:40.039
I wasn't family.

00:00:40.039 --> 00:00:42.709
-Please welcome back to the
show, our friend Hong Chau.

00:00:42.709 --> 00:00:44.911
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:44.911 --> 00:00:52.819 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:52.819 --> 00:01:00.760 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:00.760 --> 00:01:03.396
-Hello, Hong,
welcome back to the show!

00:01:03.396 --> 00:01:05.932
-I'm so happy to see you.
-I'm so happy to be back.

00:01:05.932 --> 00:01:07.533
I was -- I was telling people
backstage

00:01:07.533 --> 00:01:09.802
I was so nervous
the first time I was here,

00:01:09.802 --> 00:01:11.704
and you were so great
and so nice.

00:01:11.704 --> 00:01:13.172
-All right, great, and now
you're feeling --

00:01:13.172 --> 00:01:14.807
hopefully you're feeling
relaxed and ready to go.

00:01:14.807 --> 00:01:16.041
-Yes, it wasn't you.

00:01:16.041 --> 00:01:19.078
I was afraid that you were
going to ask me if I had

00:01:19.078 --> 00:01:21.513
played any pranks on set.
-Oh, yeah.

00:01:21.513 --> 00:01:22.982
Are you not a prankster?
-No.

00:01:22.982 --> 00:01:24.784
-Yeah.
-No, that would have been bad.

00:01:24.784 --> 00:01:29.054
"Hey, mom and dad, I just booked
this really wonderful role,

00:01:29.054 --> 00:01:32.224
but I got fired because I just
could not help myself

00:01:32.224 --> 00:01:34.760
from putting shaving cream in
Matt Damon's shoes."

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:36.161
[ Light laughter ]
-Yeah, that's right.

00:01:36.161 --> 00:01:38.564
-That is true.
So, "downsizing" was, I believe

00:01:38.564 --> 00:01:40.867
the first time
many of us saw you,

00:01:40.867 --> 00:01:42.935
and since then
your career has been,

00:01:42.935 --> 00:01:44.904
I would imagine "easier"
is the wrong word.

00:01:44.904 --> 00:01:46.939
but certainly parts
are coming easier.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:49.141
Do you still remember the time
before "Downsizing?"

00:01:49.141 --> 00:01:51.643
What it was like to be an actor?
-Oh, yeah, absolutely.

00:01:51.643 --> 00:01:53.746
It took me 10 years to become
an overnight success.

00:01:53.746 --> 00:01:57.217
So I definitely --
I still have the pay stub

00:01:57.217 --> 00:01:59.552
from when I was a cater waiter.
-Really?

00:01:59.552 --> 00:02:01.754
You kept your
cater waiter pay stub?

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:04.390
-Yes, as a reminder
of that nightmare.

00:02:04.390 --> 00:02:06.392
[ Laughter ]
And I never returned to it.

00:02:06.392 --> 00:02:09.562
-You went to B.U.
what were you studying at B.U.?

00:02:09.562 --> 00:02:11.397
-I actually studied
film production.

00:02:11.397 --> 00:02:12.999
-So you thought you'd
be behind the camera?

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:14.901
-Yeah, yeah.
I never wanted to be an actor.

00:02:14.901 --> 00:02:17.803
I actually was really
introverted and had a hard time

00:02:17.803 --> 00:02:19.438
just talking to people, so I --

00:02:19.438 --> 00:02:20.806
-Yeah, so that's hard
for actors.

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:21.874
Yes.

00:02:21.874 --> 00:02:23.342
[ Laughter ]

00:02:23.342 --> 00:02:24.911
Yeah, it's a skill.
-And, so, what happened?

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.613
When was the shift that you
realized

00:02:26.613 --> 00:02:28.114
"Oh, acting's what
I want to do now?"

00:02:28.114 --> 00:02:30.984
-Um, I just recognized that,
you know,

00:02:30.984 --> 00:02:32.151
I needed to work on myself,

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:34.554
so I started to take public
speaking classes,

00:02:34.554 --> 00:02:36.089
and then that led
to improv classes,

00:02:36.089 --> 00:02:38.491
and I wanted to barf before
every improv class,

00:02:38.491 --> 00:02:41.928
but I forced myself to go
and here I am.

00:02:41.928 --> 00:02:43.830
-Here you are.
It all worked out.

00:02:43.830 --> 00:02:45.231
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:02:45.231 --> 00:02:48.434
This is a film, almost
recognizable,

00:02:48.434 --> 00:02:50.003
that's Brendan Fraser in the
clip,

00:02:50.003 --> 00:02:52.237
and he's playing a man
struggling with obesity.

00:02:52.237 --> 00:02:53.406
You're his caretaker.

00:02:53.406 --> 00:02:55.107
This film has received
a ton of attention.

00:02:55.107 --> 00:02:56.376
It was at Venice.

00:02:56.376 --> 00:03:00.112
What's it like being in a Darren
Aronofsky film, no less,

00:03:00.112 --> 00:03:02.281
that's receiving
so much attention?

00:03:02.281 --> 00:03:04.616
-Absolutely incredible.

00:03:04.616 --> 00:03:07.153
We all had
so many doubts going in.

00:03:07.153 --> 00:03:08.754
You know, every movie is a big
leap of faith,

00:03:08.754 --> 00:03:14.159
but we shot this in February
of 2021 during Covid,

00:03:14.159 --> 00:03:16.963
before vaccines were available,
so that was still

00:03:16.963 --> 00:03:18.431
a very scary time.

00:03:18.431 --> 00:03:24.671
And then Darren Aronofsky is
this really acclaimed director,

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.941
and you have no idea how an
auteur director is going to be

00:03:27.941 --> 00:03:30.443
when you work with him, and he
was just so sweet and open,

00:03:30.443 --> 00:03:32.911
and then Brendan Fraser
is just an angel.

00:03:32.911 --> 00:03:33.913
You've worked with him,
you know.

00:03:33.913 --> 00:03:35.181
-I have.

00:03:35.181 --> 00:03:37.517
I worked with him on a film that
received less attention.

00:03:37.517 --> 00:03:39.018
[ Laughter ]

00:03:39.018 --> 00:03:40.353
I worked with him on a film
called

00:03:40.353 --> 00:03:41.554
"Journey to the Center
of the Earth."

00:03:41.554 --> 00:03:42.855
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:42.855 --> 00:03:44.223
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:44.223 --> 00:03:46.258
-Yeah.

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:47.860
-Don't patronize me.

00:03:47.860 --> 00:03:49.862
[ Laughter ]

00:03:49.862 --> 00:03:51.430
But he is so lovely.
-He is.

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:53.299
-He is so lovely.

00:03:53.299 --> 00:03:56.068
And this, you know, I'm
wondering, had you known him?

00:03:56.068 --> 00:03:57.370
Were you somebody when you --

00:03:57.370 --> 00:03:59.405
were you watching
Brendan Fraser movies?

00:03:59.405 --> 00:04:03.108
-No, I actually had not seen
any of his movies

00:04:03.108 --> 00:04:04.810
prior to working with him.

00:04:04.810 --> 00:04:07.413
And normally I would do some
research,

00:04:07.413 --> 00:04:10.816
but I just had a baby
right beforehand, so, you know,

00:04:10.816 --> 00:04:12.885
you know how it is.
-Yeah, absolutely.

00:04:12.885 --> 00:04:15.921
And, so, you showed up on set
and you knew Brendan Fraser

00:04:15.921 --> 00:04:18.423
was a guy named Brendan Fraser
and that's about it?

00:04:18.423 --> 00:04:20.526
-That's -- I mean,
I knew he was --

00:04:20.526 --> 00:04:21.827
-Tight, right.
-You know.

00:04:21.827 --> 00:04:24.796
I knew he was a hunk,
and was famous, and all of that,

00:04:24.796 --> 00:04:26.198
but yeah, it's weird,

00:04:26.198 --> 00:04:28.267
I'm definitely in the right
age group to be a superfan.

00:04:28.267 --> 00:04:31.270
But I'm glad I got to meet him
the way that I did, yeah.

00:04:31.270 --> 00:04:32.805
-This film is in Venice.

00:04:32.805 --> 00:04:35.174
Have you been to Venice before
with anything?

00:04:35.174 --> 00:04:37.176
-With "Downsizing," actually.
-Oh, nice.

00:04:37.176 --> 00:04:38.711
How was your Venice experience
this time?

00:04:38.711 --> 00:04:41.381
-It was my second time, but
the first time with Brendan.

00:04:41.381 --> 00:04:42.849
-Yep.
-And that was amazing because

00:04:42.849 --> 00:04:45.284
the audience gave him like,
a six, seven minute

00:04:45.284 --> 00:04:47.586
standing ovation.
It was really, really moving.

00:04:47.586 --> 00:04:50.556
I'm so glad that audiences
get to see him again

00:04:50.556 --> 00:04:52.692
in this really incredible role.

00:04:52.692 --> 00:04:56.929
-You also were in a fantastic
film that's still in theatres

00:04:56.929 --> 00:04:59.298
called "The Menu," and you were
great in "The Menu."

00:04:59.298 --> 00:05:01.234
And we're big fans of
"The Menu" at the show

00:05:01.234 --> 00:05:04.437
because it was co-written by one
of our writers, Seth Reiss --

00:05:04.437 --> 00:05:06.205
-
with his writing partner
Will Tracy.

00:05:06.205 --> 00:05:08.206
And was this a role when you
first read the script,

00:05:08.206 --> 00:05:11.377
did you think that this was
a perfect role for you?

00:05:11.377 --> 00:05:13.279
-Yes, because the description
was

00:05:13.279 --> 00:05:15.081
"Severe Scandinavian woman."

00:05:15.081 --> 00:05:20.820
[ Laughter ]

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.555
-That's you.
-I got this. Yes.

00:05:22.555 --> 00:05:24.356
-So what happens?
what is the path?

00:05:24.356 --> 00:05:26.258
I guess the director, at some
point says, you know,

00:05:26.258 --> 00:05:28.727
that part's less -- I mean,
by the way, you know,

00:05:28.727 --> 00:05:30.396
Reiss writes stuff all the time

00:05:30.396 --> 00:05:31.931
that we ignore here,
as well, so...

00:05:31.931 --> 00:05:33.032
[ Laughter ]

00:05:33.032 --> 00:05:36.102
-Well, I did ask to cut
some of my lines.

00:05:36.102 --> 00:05:37.536
-Did you really?
-I did.

00:05:37.536 --> 00:05:39.238
-Yeah, he does overwrite,
doesn't he?

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.440
[ Laughter ]
-yeah.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:42.841
It's a problem.
It's a problem.

00:05:42.841 --> 00:05:45.010
-I mean, I would li-- If you
could come in and give our staff

00:05:45.010 --> 00:05:47.279
a tutorial on how to
cut his stuff.

00:05:47.279 --> 00:05:48.714
-Give me a red pen, I'm ready.

00:05:48.714 --> 00:05:50.416
[ Laughter ]

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.085
-And, so, you had --
we were talking about,

00:05:52.085 --> 00:05:54.253
you had a baby during covid.

00:05:54.253 --> 00:05:56.723
-Yes. I found out I was pregnant

00:05:56.723 --> 00:05:58.958
a few days before
the lockdown happened,

00:05:58.958 --> 00:06:01.093
and it was actually a really
nice time for me,

00:06:01.093 --> 00:06:02.728
despite all of the horror
going on.

00:06:02.728 --> 00:06:06.665
-Yeah, I mean, there is
something about being able

00:06:06.665 --> 00:06:09.568
to, you know, remove yourself
from the daily grind

00:06:09.568 --> 00:06:11.770
during a time when it is, you
know, I think,

00:06:11.770 --> 00:06:14.173
you should be able to,
like, nest and prepare.

00:06:14.173 --> 00:06:15.708
-It was so nice.
I didn't feel like I was missing

00:06:15.708 --> 00:06:16.842
out on anything.

00:06:16.842 --> 00:06:20.145
I could just ignore all of my
emails,

00:06:20.145 --> 00:06:22.515
and just not pick up my phone.
It was great.

00:06:22.515 --> 00:06:24.116
-That's great.
And she's almost 2 now?

00:06:24.116 --> 00:06:27.085
-She just turned 2.
-Oh, my God, fantastic.

00:06:27.085 --> 00:06:29.355
So the personality I would
imagine is popping.?

00:06:29.355 --> 00:06:33.158
-Yes, she's --
she's singing her ABCs,

00:06:33.158 --> 00:06:34.627
and "Twinkle Twinkle
Little Star,"

00:06:34.627 --> 00:06:38.097
and afterwards, she says, "'ank
you, everybody, 'ank you."

00:06:38.097 --> 00:06:40.332
[ Laughter ]

00:06:40.332 --> 00:06:41.601
-Where did she learn --

00:06:41.601 --> 00:06:45.171
Oh, she's not going to need
public speaking classes.

00:06:45.171 --> 00:06:46.605
She's ready to go.

00:06:46.605 --> 00:06:48.541
What is there -- do you have any
work yet

00:06:48.541 --> 00:06:49.675
that you think are gonna be --

00:06:49.675 --> 00:06:51.076
how old is your daughter
gonna have to be

00:06:51.076 --> 00:06:52.378
before she sees anything?

00:06:52.378 --> 00:06:53.512
I feel like I can't think of
anything that's not like,

00:06:53.512 --> 00:06:54.880
17-years-old.
-A long time.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:57.749
So, if there's a family movie
that somebody's looking

00:06:57.749 --> 00:07:01.053
for a severe Scandinavian woman
to star in...

00:07:01.053 --> 00:07:02.888
[ Laughter ]
I'm your girl.

00:07:02.888 --> 00:07:05.958
-Have you gone on your head shot
now, put special skills,

00:07:05.958 --> 00:07:07.727
"Plays severe Scandinavian?"

00:07:07.727 --> 00:07:09.595
-I should, I should,
thanks, sir, for that.

00:07:09.595 --> 00:07:11.097
-Did you -- wait,
so "The Whale," did you --

00:07:11.097 --> 00:07:12.965
'cause I imagine you don't have
to audition much anymore,

00:07:12.965 --> 00:07:14.299
but did you, for "The Whale?"

00:07:14.299 --> 00:07:15.668
-I did.
-Wow.

00:07:15.668 --> 00:07:17.169
-I did have to audition
for "The Whale."

00:07:17.169 --> 00:07:19.939
Because I'm highfalutin now
and I don't have to do that.

00:07:19.939 --> 00:07:22.474
but I did have to because it's
Darren Aronofsky.

00:07:22.474 --> 00:07:23.976
-It's Darren Aronofsky,
he's very particular.

00:07:23.976 --> 00:07:25.477
Obviously it shows up
on the screen.

00:07:25.477 --> 00:07:26.845
When he knows what he wants,
he gets it.

00:07:26.845 --> 00:07:29.147
But how -- In this day and age
is that something

00:07:29.147 --> 00:07:31.417
where you have to like,
film yourself, or...?

00:07:31.417 --> 00:07:34.053
-It was very difficult with the
baby,

00:07:34.053 --> 00:07:38.190
because I couldn't use my
non-mommy voice with her.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:41.694
So whenever I would try to do
the audition tape,

00:07:41.694 --> 00:07:44.930
I'd use my deeper adult voice
and she would start crying,

00:07:44.930 --> 00:07:48.100
and so you'd hear this crying
baby in the background.

00:07:48.100 --> 00:07:50.702
And we managed to get one clean
take,

00:07:50.702 --> 00:07:52.304
and that was the one
that we sent off.

00:07:52.304 --> 00:07:54.940
-Yeah, 'cause based on the clip
I don't think that scene would

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.243
have the same power if you were
using a mommy voice.

00:07:57.243 --> 00:08:00.312
[ Laughter ]

00:08:00.312 --> 00:08:01.780
-Very, very different.
-And what about the holidays?

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:03.082
Are you --
do you guys have plans?

00:08:03.082 --> 00:08:04.417
Are you excited?

00:08:04.417 --> 00:08:06.419
-Um, I'm just ready to rest,

00:08:06.419 --> 00:08:08.654
I feel like I've been
really busy working.

00:08:08.654 --> 00:08:10.222
I just wrapped up a movie
in New Orleans,

00:08:10.222 --> 00:08:13.593
which is my hometown, so I was
very excited to do that.

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:16.228
-Is there added pressure when
you're working in your hometown?

00:08:16.228 --> 00:08:18.997
Do you have to, like, the manage
your family and friends?

00:08:18.997 --> 00:08:21.934
-Well, no, it's more about
people asking for

00:08:21.934 --> 00:08:23.669
restaurant recommendations.
-Yeah.

00:08:23.669 --> 00:08:24.970
[ Laughter ]

00:08:24.970 --> 00:08:27.006
That's oppressive when they --

00:08:27.006 --> 00:08:29.074
-It is, like, "What's the best
Vietnamese restaurant?"

00:08:29.074 --> 00:08:31.243
or "What's the best --" you
know, whatever you go to.

00:08:31.243 --> 00:08:32.577
-yeah, 'cause you don't want to
work with people all day,

00:08:32.577 --> 00:08:34.380
and then know the next morning
you're gonna find out

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:36.414
what they think about you
as a concierge.

00:08:36.414 --> 00:08:37.883
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:08:37.883 --> 00:08:41.387
-They're like, "Eh, I guess we
have different tastes, Hong."

00:08:41.387 --> 00:08:43.355
[ Laughter ]

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:45.091
"Not my favorite."

00:08:45.091 --> 00:08:48.093
2 years old, does your daughter
have an awareness of Christmas?

00:08:48.093 --> 00:08:50.429
Do you feel like that,
you at least have?

00:08:50.429 --> 00:08:54.500
-Well, I do read "Elmo's 12 Days
of Christmas" to her

00:08:54.500 --> 00:08:56.535
over and over and over.
-gotcha.

00:08:56.535 --> 00:08:58.404
-So I think she knows that.
-Got it.

00:08:58.404 --> 00:09:00.706
Is Elmo her favorite
of the "Sesame Street"?

00:09:00.706 --> 00:09:01.707
-Yeah. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:09:01.707 --> 00:09:03.976
-well, actually Abby is now --

00:09:03.976 --> 00:09:06.312
-Oh, yeah.
-Scooching up on the list.

00:09:06.312 --> 00:09:08.413
But I wasn't familiar with Abby
when I was growing up.

00:09:08.413 --> 00:09:10.115
-I think Abby came after our
time, yeah.

00:09:10.115 --> 00:09:11.917
I had an interesting thing
happen,

00:09:11.917 --> 00:09:13.986
which is I have some friends who
work at "Sesame Street,"

00:09:13.986 --> 00:09:15.855
and when my second was --
-[ Gasps ] Can I get on?

00:09:15.855 --> 00:09:16.822
-What?
-Can I get on?

00:09:16.822 --> 00:09:19.425
-I'm sorry, we're not taking --

00:09:19.425 --> 00:09:21.360
[ Laughter ]

00:09:21.360 --> 00:09:22.995
We'd love for you
to get yourself on tape.

00:09:22.995 --> 00:09:24.863
[ Laughter ]

00:09:24.863 --> 00:09:25.830
We'd love to get you on there.

00:09:25.830 --> 00:09:27.934
But I did -- we, uh...

00:09:27.934 --> 00:09:30.636
so friends of mine work there,
and when my second was born,

00:09:30.636 --> 00:09:33.805
they obviously know the
psychology of the first,

00:09:33.805 --> 00:09:35.474
maybe might be intimidated
by the second.

00:09:35.474 --> 00:09:38.077
And, so, they did this really
sweet thing where may had Elmo

00:09:38.077 --> 00:09:39.545
record a video saying, like

00:09:39.545 --> 00:09:41.547
"Hey, Ash, you're about to have
a baby brother.

00:09:41.547 --> 00:09:42.848
Congratulations!"

00:09:42.848 --> 00:09:45.083
And it was so -- and my son who
was 2 at the time,

00:09:45.083 --> 00:09:48.154
watched it, and his
reaction was horror.

00:09:48.154 --> 00:09:49.622
[ Laughter ]

00:09:49.622 --> 00:09:51.022
Like, I think every part of him
was like,

00:09:51.022 --> 00:09:54.026
why does this [ Bleep ] Puppet
know so much about me?

00:09:54.026 --> 00:09:55.361
[ Laughter ]

00:09:55.361 --> 00:09:58.597
It was like,
"Well, he knows my name.

00:09:58.597 --> 00:10:02.468
He knows mom's having a ba--
I don't like this guy."

00:10:02.468 --> 00:10:04.436
So just know, it cuts both ways.

00:10:04.436 --> 00:10:05.937
Cuts both ways.

00:10:05.937 --> 00:10:07.239
Hong, thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:07.239 --> 00:10:08.741
Congrats on the film.
-Thank you.

00:10:08.741 --> 00:10:11.543
-Happy holidays and come back
again soon.

00:10:11.543 --> 00:10:12.578
Hong Chau, everybody.

00:10:12.578 --> 00:10:14.413
"The Whale" is in select
theaters tomorrow

00:10:14.413 --> 00:10:16.215
and opens nationwide
December 21st.

00:10:16.215 --> 00:10:17.917
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night."

